  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the probability of the maximum outcome of each lottery and prefers the one with the higher such probability. Lottery B has a higher probability (100%) of its maximum outcome than Lottery A (80%), so the reason would favor B. The individual does consider the probability of the maximum outcome of each lottery — explicitly mentioning 80% for 4000 Euros and referencing the 100% for 3000 Euros. However, their justification leads them to forgo the certain outcome in favor of the 80% chance, implying a preference for A. This suggests they did not follow the rule of the decision reason (which would pick B), but they did engage with the same informational elements — the probabilities of the maximum outcomes. The comparison shows that while the individual considered the relevant information (probabilities of maximum outcomes), their decision rule was not aligned with the reason's rule (preferring higher probability of maximum outcome). Instead, they seem to find the 80% chance sufficiently high to justify giving up certainty, which is a different rationale. Therefore, although the information matches partially, the rule does not align.",
